      "knowledge_point": "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant with Special Angles",
      "problem": "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\text{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the  first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/4cbf970747a9eddbcffe6fd3ba46d13c43e5f17ca56e52747c797229dbc74d48.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$",
